    {
      "question": "e-13169",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "5de899a57d21ba3e22a22eb70253669ab3eda0ffb4e59a12f24da54197215028",
      "new_sha256": "29337f86a7a179842e99e78f5d0da0247d3bcf9d021a8ebb920d465727a693ee",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-13169",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The cardinality of a finite set $A$ is the number of elements in $A.$ \n\nNotice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n10, \\quad 19, \\quad 28,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with a common difference $d=9{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =9 \\\\  & =19-10 \\\\  & =28-19 \\\\  & =\u22ef\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our arithmetic sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =10+(n-1)\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =10+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $A$ in set-builder notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={9n+1:n\\in N,\\,1\\le n\\le N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N\\in \\mathbb N$ is to be determined.\n\nTo find $N,$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9N+1 & =865 \\\\ 9N & =864 \\\\ N & =\\frac{864}{9} \\\\ N & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our set $A$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\nA = \\left\\{9n+1: n\\in \\mathbb N,\\: 1\\leq n \\leq 96  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $A$ has $96$ elements, we must have $|A| = 96.$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040149/example-13169.json",
          "file_sha256": "81d7a69d2a2bddb9bd2f47ddbf1067f8a6567e92f3925aa1e2d7d764907c1390"
        }
